 "drivingQuestion": "How large must the remnant excluded from a city's eiruv be?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "R' Yitzḥak's wording is emended; the Rav/R' Yitzḥak disagreement stands",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "eruvin.60a.16",
   "daf": "60a",
   "sentenceId": 15,
   "he": "רַבִּי שִׁמְעוֹן אוֹמֵר שָׁלֹשׁ חֲצֵירוֹת וְכוּ׳. אָמַר רַב חָמָא בַּר גּוּרְיָא אָמַר רַב: הֲלָכָה כְּרַבִּי שִׁמְעוֹן. רַבִּי יִצְחָק אָמַר: אֲפִילּוּ בַּיִת אֶחָד וְחָצֵר אַחַת. חָצֵר אַחַת סָלְקָא דַּעְתָּךְ?! אֶלָּא אֵימָא: בַּיִת אֶחָד בְּחָצֵר אַחַת.",
   "en": "It is stated in the mishna that «Rabbi Shimon says:» The excluded area must be large enough to include at least «three courtyards» with two houses each. «Rav Ḥama bar Gurya said» that «Rav said:» The «halakha is in accordance with» the opinion of «Rabbi Shimon.» However, «Rabbi Yitzḥak said: Even one house and one courtyard» suffice. The Gemara expresses surprise at the wording of this statement: «Can it enter your mind» that «one courtyard» even without a house is sufficient? «Rather,» correct it and «say» as follows: «One house in one courtyard.»"
  }
 ],
